there is new political chaos in Spain  this morning the country's Prime  Minister called on the government to  approve direct rule over Catalonia the  move follows the region's disputed vote  for independence earlier this month  Seth Doane is in Barcelona that's  Catalonia is capital south good morning  good morning it may seem quiet out in  front of the Parliament but inside the  building behind me catalonia's  Parliament an intense debate is underway  as we speak they are looking at whether  they may declare independence  finally and outside these gates you have  supporters who have gathered it has the  feeling of a kind of mix between a  protest and a sort of street party you  see people with the flags that have been  adopted by the independence movement  here in Catalonia people wearing  democracy logos people saying that they  are protecting the Parliament supporters  gathered around it cheering on  parliamentarians and mayors as they come  in to listen to this key vote on the  other side of course this morning we had  Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy the prime  minister of Spain who made an  impassioned speech in front of Spain's  Senate he was trying to make the  argument for article 155 that never  before a used piece of Spain's  Constitution which would allow for  direct rule here in Catalonia he said it  was important to restore law and order  here and also democracy one of the  things that would come with that if  Mariano Rajoy gets his way is you would  see some of the top leaders in Catalonia  sacked they would see the President of  the Catalonian region lose his job and  the Parliament here  lose some of their power there is a  great deal of fear all of this  surrounding the violence that sparked  around that October first referendum and  today you find people outside around  Parliament not far from where I'm  standing now who really worry about  Spain coming in and imposing direct rule  and some separatist leaders Noora have  vowed civil disobedience if indeed Spain  enters an important story there Seth  Doane in Barcelona thank you
 

